1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,240
This is an introduction to KERI, the Key
Event Receipt Infrastructure.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:13,120
My name is Samuel Smith and I am the
inventor of KERI. The reason I invented

3
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:19,040
KERI is because the internet is broken and
I wanted to solve it. And so the problem

4
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:24,360
that KERI solves is called the universal
secure attribution problem. Okay, what do

5
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:30,060
we mean by universal secure attribution
problem? Well, universal means everywhere.

6
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:39,220
We want it to be solved everywhere. And
secure means do it in a way that can't be

7
00:00:39,220 --> 00:00:47,420
hacked or forged or impersonated or
attacked in the normal way that we get

8
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:53,720
attacked on the internet. Attribution
means assigning a communication to its

9
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:58,420
source. We want to attribute where
something came from to its source. We know

10
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:04,980
who's sending it to us. And so the problem
is, how do we do that everywhere on the

11
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:09,960
internet in a secure way so that we know
who's sending us information? So that

12
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:16,840
doesn't mean we want to enable anybody to
track everybody's conversations. We want

13
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:23,520
to do it in a privacy protecting way. So
that poses additional challenges. But

14
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:28,700
first we have to get the security down.
Many people approach this problem and they

15
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:33,100
sacrifice security. And if you sacrifice
the security, then you don't have privacy

16
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:37,900
either. So, what we want to do is solve
all of these problems at the same time in

17
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:45,320
a very integrated, elegant, simple way.
Let's talk about what it means. Secure

18
00:01:45,320 --> 00:01:51,440
attribution is another way of saying we
want to establish the authorship of data,

19
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:55,900
any and all data sent over the internet,
any and all documents, things like

20
00:01:55,900 --> 00:02:02,720
credentials, entitlements, authorizations.
We want to be able to say that we can

21
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:08,700
securely attribute who's doing what in a
privacy-preserving way. And I keep saying

22
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:12,760
privacy because I don't want to give the
false impression that we're trying to

23
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:18,180
build this universal tracking system. The
hard problem is to be able to do this all

24
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:26,200
in a way that keeps confidentiality and
privacy along with secure attribution.

25
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:32,020
That's always been the hard problem. So to
restate, the problem is we want to have

26
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:38,440
verifiable secure attribution of any
communications to its source. Now I added

27
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:42,680
the word verifiable. What does verifiable
mean? It means that if we're receiving

28
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:48,820
information, we need some way to verify
where it came from. When I say verify, it

29
00:02:48,820 --> 00:02:54,280
means in a cryptographic sense. It means
doing it without having to trust any third

30
00:02:54,280 --> 00:03:00,180
party. I don't want to have to trust
somebody else to verify what somebody that

31
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:05,480
I want to communicate with in a private
confidential way says. I need to be able

32
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:11,680
to do it by myself. If I have to trust
someone, then the security of whoever I'm

33
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:16,600
trusting now becomes something I have to
worry about. And that is a problem.

34
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:23,980
Another way of looking at secure
attribution is to say that we want to have

35
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:32,140
provenance of any data by anyone, to
anyone, from anyone. And so a way we can

36
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:37,540
describe that is as authentic data. And
then we want authentic data provenance.

37
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:42,740
Authentic has two meanings. It means like
true to yourself, like you're being

38
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:46,940
authentic. That's not the meaning of
authentic we're using here. We're meaning

39
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:52,820
authentic as you can attribute it to its
source. Who painted that painting is an

40
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:57,720
authentic painting, right? As opposed to
when something's not authentic. It means

41
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:03,600
it was forged or somebody was
impersonated. Somebody sends you

42
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:10,400
information and they say it came from Joe
when it really came from Sue. And so the

43
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:16,260
whole problem is back to the same thing.
We need to securely attribute the source

44
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:21,460
of information. So what do I mean by
provenance? We often hear the term

45
00:04:21,460 --> 00:04:26,300
provenance when we talk about like a
painting, like fine art, right? Was it

46
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:31,900
painted by Rembrandt? And how do you know
it was painted by Rembrandt? Well, one way

47
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:38,080
that you would provenance it is that you
would establish a chain of custody of that

48
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:43,200
painting from Rembrandt to whoever
Rembrandt sold or gave the painting to

49
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:49,300
whoever sold or gave the painting and so
on. And if you can follow that chain of

50
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:54,820
custody to the present owner or holder of
that painting, then you can say, yeah, it

51
00:04:54,820 --> 00:05:02,640
came from Rembrandt. So if we can solve
data provenance, then we can solve many of

52
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:08,420
the security problems on the internet
because most of them come from the fact

53
00:05:08,420 --> 00:05:14,700
that we can't solve data provenance. We
don't have universal secure attribution.

54
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:19,400
And because we don't have universal secure
attribution, people can forge things.

55
00:05:19,540 --> 00:05:24,280
People can impersonate things. Well, when
we send data over the internet, we have a

56
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:30,260
similar problem. Somebody sends a message.
It gets sent to some server in the cloud

57
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:34,680
who then sends it to another server, who
routes it to another server, who routes it

58
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:40,360
to another server, and then it ends up in
your inbox. And you go, did it come from

59
00:05:40,360 --> 00:05:45,400
where it said it came from? Anybody that
was in the path could have intercepted it,

60
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,920
spoofed it, done something to it, and then
it shows up in your box and it says, oh

61
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:56,340
yeah, this came from Sue, but it really
came from Bob. So provenance is about

62
00:05:56,340 --> 00:06:04,240
establishing secure attribution even
though the data traverses multiple hosts

63
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:08,980
or servers on the internet. It didn't come
directly to us. It wasn't given to us in

64
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:13,820
person. It came over the internet and the
internet is public. The internet is public

65
00:06:13,820 --> 00:06:18,520
infrastructure. It's public, which means
that lots of people have the ability to

66
00:06:18,520 --> 00:06:22,720
see what you're sending or to change what
you're sending or to mess with it. And

67
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:25,820
many of the attacks on the internet come
from the fact that it's public

68
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:31,000
infrastructure and therefore publicly
accessible for attackers to attack. So we

69
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,880
want to solve this secure attribution
problem. Let's dig a little deeper. On the

70
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:41,740
internet, we're just sending information.
Data is information. It's just bits. Now

71
00:06:41,740 --> 00:06:45,620
we talked about chain of custody. When we
normally think of chain of custody, we

72
00:06:45,620 --> 00:06:50,540
think of a physical item. So I'll call it
those its. They're atoms. So if I actually

73
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:54,440
have a painting, there's only one of them.
And it takes a lot of work to copy that

74
00:06:54,440 --> 00:07:00,420
painting. And if I have two, then I have a
problem because somebody made a copy. How

75
00:07:00,420 --> 00:07:05,380
do you tell the original from the copy?
Well, if the copy is done really, really

76
00:07:05,380 --> 00:07:09,880
well, just looking at the two, you can't
tell the original from the copy. That's

77
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:14,520
why you have to have a chain of custody.
You say the original was in possession of

78
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:20,640
somebody and they gave it to somebody else
and they gave it to somebody else. And the

79
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:24,900
copy was never in their possession.
possession. So you know it's a copy

80
00:07:24,900 --> 00:07:29,180
because it wasn't part of the chain of
custody. That works for atoms. It doesn't

81
00:07:29,180 --> 00:07:34,500
work for information bits. So we have a
harder problem to provenance data

82
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:38,560
information. So we have to use
cryptography to do that. One of the first

83
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:45,600
things we do with cryptography is that we
give an identifier to the source. The

84
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:49,780
source now has an identifier. That
identifier is an electronic identifier.

85
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:56,980
It's more information. It's more bits. To
make the ability to security attribute to

86
00:07:56,980 --> 00:08:02,140
a source, that identifier needs to be
unique. And in order to have universal

87
00:08:02,140 --> 00:08:07,660
secure attribution, that identifier needs
to be universally unique. There can only

88
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:13,940
be one in the whole universe. The only way
that we can guarantee your universally

89
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:21,340
unique identifiers is to use cryptography
or to use some trusted third party who

90
00:08:21,340 --> 00:08:27,100
creates them. Well, we don't want to have
to rely on a trusted third party, so the

91
00:08:27,100 --> 00:08:31,840
identifiers must be cryptographic in
nature. So, let's suppose that we have a

92
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:38,100
system for generating cryptographic
identifiers. Now, we must be able to prove

93
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:42,700
who controls that identifier, and we have
to do it in a verifiable way without

94
00:08:42,700 --> 00:08:48,740
trusting the third party. If I have a
cryptographic identifier and a way to

95
00:08:48,740 --> 00:08:54,700
verifiably prove controller over that
identifier, then I have what is called a

96
00:08:54,700 --> 00:09:01,800
root of trust. I have the ability now to
attribute data to that identifier where

97
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:06,900
that identifier is the source of that
data. So, I now have constructed the basic

98
00:09:06,900 --> 00:09:13,080
problem. The way that I can control an
identifier or prove control of an

99
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:17,960
identifier is to use what we call
cryptographic keys. And the specific type

100
00:09:17,960 --> 00:09:24,240
of key that we use belongs to something
called a public key pair or a public

101
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:30,380
private key pair where I have a public key
and a private key. That is going to be in

102
00:09:30,380 --> 00:09:37,140
a completely new subject to talk about the
basics of public private key pairs. But

103
00:09:37,140 --> 00:09:41,820
what we've done is set up the core idea of
how we get to universally secure

104
00:09:41,820 --> 00:09:46,220
attribution. And we haven't even really
talked about how KERI does it, but I've

105
00:09:46,220 --> 00:09:51,180
given you an introduction to the hard
problem. The hard problem is universal

106
00:09:51,180 --> 00:09:56,900
secure attribution. Follow along to learn
more. Leave any questions in the comments.

107
00:09:57,860 --> 00:09:58,100
Thank you.

108
00:10:27,780 --> 00:10:28,500
Thank you.

